# Tarantula agent reference

You are working with Tarantula, an agent-native CLI for small full-stack apps on Cloudflare Workers, Static Assets, and D1.

## Read the source of truth

Base URL: https://tarantula-9l0.pages.dev

1. Fetch `/llms.txt` for the documentation map.
2. Fetch `/docs.json` when you need a machine-readable page manifest.
3. Read the relevant Markdown page under `/docs/<slug>/index.md` before changing an app or running a remote operation.
4. Use `/llms-full.txt` only when the task crosses several product areas.

Do not treat planned features as available. The docs label every feature as available, mixed, or planned.

## Preserve the contract

- `tarantula.json` is the app-owned, provider-neutral source of truth.
- `tarantula.lock.json` stores stable, non-secret remote identities and should be committed.
- `.tarantula/wrangler.jsonc` is generated. Never edit it.
- Migrations are ordered and immutable after application. Add the next migration instead of rewriting an applied one.

## Work safely

1. Read `tarantula.json` and the relevant docs.
2. Run `tarantula doctor --json` before deployment work.
3. Use `tarantula plan --json` to see what a deploy would change. It never creates, migrates, or deploys anything.
4. Use `tarantula deploy --dry-run` before a remote deploy when validating changes.
5. Use `tarantula deploy --json` only when the user asked to deploy.
6. Verify remote state with `tarantula inspect --json`.
7. Use `tarantula drift --json` to check whether the provider still matches `tarantula.lock.json`.
8. Use `tarantula logs` for live request outcomes.

Exit codes: `0` success, `1` error (including a `plan` blocked by an unowned name conflict), `2` only from `drift` when the provider no longer matches the lockfile. Treat `2` as a state to report, not a crash.

The v0 chat template is public by default: anyone with its URL can read and post. Never describe it as private or suitable for secrets.

## Instant hosting (no Cloudflare account)

`tarantula deploy` falls back to Tarantula instant hosting when no Cloudflare account is detected, and `--instant` forces it. The app lands on a real public URL owned by Tarantula's account. The claim token is printed once, by the deploy that mints it; it is never written to disk and redeploys never reprint it. Pass it to the user verbatim.

```sh
tarantula deploy --instant --json
tarantula claim <token> --json
```

```json
{
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "status": "deployed",
  "mode": "instant",
  "name": "open-chat",
  "url": "https://i-3f9a2c81be.<subdomain>.workers.dev",
  "appId": "3f9a2c81be",
  "claimToken": "...",
  "expiresAt": 1790000000000,
  "resources": { "tables": { "name": "i-3f9a2c81be-tables" } }
}
```

```json
{ "schemaVersion": 1, "status": "claimed", "appId": "3f9a2c81be", "url": "https://i-3f9a2c81be.<subdomain>.workers.dev" }
```

`claimToken` and `expiresAt` (epoch milliseconds) are present only on the deploy that creates the app; later deploys reuse `.tarantula/instant.json` and omit them. An unclaimed app is deleted 30 days after creation. Instant apps are always public: a `"visibility": "shared"` app fails with a recovery line pointing at a Cloudflare account, because instant mode has no secrets. Creates are limited to 10 per day per IP. `tarantula.lock.json` records `"mode": "instant"`, and `inspect`, `logs`, `plan`, `drift`, and `share` refuse instant locks.

## Shared visibility (Door alpha slice)

`tarantula.json` accepts `"visibility": "public"` (default) or `"visibility": "shared"`. A shared app is gated by the template's own Door module: deploy provisions a `DOOR_SESSION_SECRET` Worker secret once, and every request without a valid signed session cookie gets a 401 invitation page.

```sh
tarantula share add ana@example.com --json   # returns a single-use invite URL
tarantula share list --json                  # joined | invited | expired
tarantula share remove ana@example.com --json
```

Tarantula prints the invite URL. It does not send email; the owner shares the link. Members live in the app's own `door_members` table. This is an alpha slice, not the full Door product: no roles, teams, email delivery, or central session revocation. Do not describe a shared app as having sign-in, SSO, or roles.

## Common workflow

```sh
tarantula new open-chat --template chat
cd open-chat
tarantula dev
tarantula plan --json
tarantula deploy --json
tarantula inspect --json
tarantula drift --json
tarantula share add ana@example.com --json
```

Return the stable app URL and important resource identities from JSON output. Keep provider noise out of user-facing summaries.
